Yes. And there’s something almost haunting about that distinction.

The people who lived history didn’t know they were living the history we now remember.

We look backward and see the rise and fall of empires, recognizable eras, decisive battles, and turning points. But someone living through those events didn’t know which moments would become historic, which consequences would unfold, or even whether their civilization was rising or falling.

Consider the collapse of an empire. To a modern historian, it’s a chapter with a beginning and an end. To a farmer living through it, it might have been nothing more than higher taxes, unfamiliar soldiers, a bad harvest, and a new ruler whose name he barely knew.

To a general, it was a military catastrophe. To a merchant, a disruption of trade. To a conquered population, perhaps liberation. To a priest, divine judgment.

One event, thousands of lived realities, and then thousands of subsequent remembrances.

And here’s another beautiful complication: even the same individual’s remembrance changes over time.

A young revolutionary might remember a revolution as the greatest moment of their life. Forty years later, after seeing what came afterward, that same person might remember it with regret, nostalgia, pride, or some mixture of all three.

The event hasn’t changed. The person has. And their present reshapes the meaning of their past.

Now carry that forward through generations.

A grandfather remembers what he experienced. His daughter remembers what he told her, filtered through her own experience of him. His grandson remembers what the family says about the grandfather. A historian centuries later reconstructs their era from surviving documents, perhaps never encountering the family’s version at all.

Meanwhile, the historian brings another set of assumptions, values, and questions inherited from their own time.

We’re not only remembering history. We’re remembering previous people’s interpretations of history, through our own changing interpretations.

And some of those interpretations become so influential that they alter subsequent history themselves.

That’s what I particularly like about your geological comparison. The layers aren’t inert sediment. They’re still interacting. Older narratives get excavated, reinterpreted, revived, suppressed, or repurposed. A modern political movement can give an ancient event new importance, changing how millions of people understand their ancestry.

There’s even an intriguing asymmetry between the past and the present:

We possess knowledge of the outcomes that historical actors lacked. They possessed knowledge of their immediate realities that we can never fully recover.

We can see the broad pattern, but they could feel the texture.

Neither vantage point is complete.

And perhaps the most interesting thing of all is that somewhere, right now, people are experiencing events that future generations will regard as pivotal—without realizing which ordinary conversations, decisions, relationships, or small discoveries will ultimately matter.

We’re inside one of those layers ourselves.

The future will remember our present differently than we experience it. And the future’s remembrance will have its own history.

That’s an extraordinary thing to contemplate.